-- 366 — the company universe behind every market sector.
--
-- GENERATED FILE. Regenerate with:
--   python3 scripts/build-company-universe.py --sql-out <this file>
-- The sector -> industry mapping it reads is scripts/sector-industry-map.json,
-- curated from a Wikidata `wbsearchentities` sweep whose candidates were ranked
-- by how many companies each industry actually carries.
--
-- WHY THE COUNTS ON /research/markets HAD NOTHING BEHIND THEM. Every one of
-- migration 228's 300 rows carried `sector = NULL`, so the sector page had a
-- universe to draw and no rows in it. This file labels the records it can source
-- and adds the rest, keyed on the taxonomy name the sector page filters by.
--
-- WHAT IS AND IS NOT CLAIMED. Each row is a discovery record: a company Wikidata
-- records in an industry, with an official website, a country and an inception
-- year. `source_url` is its Wikidata entity. No row claims funding, valuation,
-- revenue or market size, and none of them is meant to reach the supplied
-- taxonomy count printed on the card.
--
-- IDEMPOTENT. `ON CONFLICT(uid) DO UPDATE` means a re-run restates the same
-- facts rather than failing, and the closing UPDATE fills in the citation for
-- the rows this file inserted (they carry a `pending` marker instead of three
-- quarters of the same literal repeated three thousand times).

-- Company universe per taxonomy sector, generated by
-- scripts/build-company-universe.py.
--
-- Idempotent: a uid that already exists keeps its row and takes the
-- sector this pull assigned it. `source_url` is written as a marker and
-- filled in from the uid by the closing UPDATE, which is why the value
-- tuples are readable.

